// TestProtocol — the wire format a generated test binary reports to `maxon test` through, written down
// HERE and nowhere else.
//
// ⭐⭐ **BOTH ENDS ARE IN THIS FILE'S REACH, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON IT IS ONE FILE.** `TestDispatcher`
// bakes these constants into the Maxon source it synthesizes, and the runner reads back what that source
// printed. A marker spelled a second time — in `stdlib/Testing.maxon`, or in the reader — would be the
// protocol written down twice, and the two would drift the first time either side moved. That is exactly
// why `__TestReport.useWireFormat` takes the wrapper and separator as ARGUMENTS rather than declaring
// them: the stdlib holds the mechanism, this holds the format.
//
// ⭐ **EVERYTHING TRAVELS ON STDERR, alongside `__TestReport.threw`**, so protocol and error reports stay
// in one stream and their ORDER is meaningful — a `threw` between a begin and an end belongs to that test.
// A test's own `print` goes to stdout and can never be mistaken for protocol.
//
// Three line shapes, each bracketed by the wrapper at BOTH ends and separated by the field separator:
//
//   <wrapper>><sep><index><wrapper>                                   a test is about to run
//   <wrapper><<sep><index><sep><pass|fail><sep><nanos><wrapper>       a test finished under its own power
//   <wrapper><errorType><sep><errorCase><sep><file><sep><line><wrapper>   `__TestReport.threw`

// Brackets both ends of a protocol line so the runner can find it among whatever the test itself wrote to
// stderr. Distinctive rather than short: this is the string a chatty test must not accidentally emit at
// both ends of one line.
export let TestProtocolWrapper = "@@maxon-test@@"

// ⭐⭐ **THE FIELD SEPARATOR AS IT MUST BE SPELLED INSIDE A MAXON STRING LITERAL — ASCII Unit Separator
// (U+001F), and the lexer wants exactly four hex digits (it rejects `\u{1f}` by name).**
//
// A control character rather than a printable delimiter because ONE field is not ours: `threw`'s
// `errorCase` is the thrown value rendered by the language's own interpolation rule, and an enum may
// declare a STRING raw value containing any punctuation a printable delimiter could have used. A control
// character is the one class of byte such a value does not contain.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS THE SPELLING, NOT THE CHARACTER**, and the two are different things at different moments:
// generated SOURCE carries these six bytes and the running binary prints one. The reader half of the
// protocol needs the character and will declare it beside this constant, with a comment tying the pair —
// which is the shape the bootstrap already has (`MaxonTestProtocol.FieldSeparator` +
// `FieldSeparatorInMaxonSource`). It is not declared yet because nothing reads it yet, and an unread
// export is a claim rather than a fact.
export let TestProtocolSeparatorInMaxonSource = "\\u001f"

// First field of a line the DISPATCHER wrote, marking a test about to run.
//
// ⚠ **`>` AND `<` DISAMBIGUATE STRUCTURALLY RATHER THAN BY HOPE.** The first field of a `threw` line is
// the callee's declared `throws` TYPE NAME, which is always a Maxon identifier, and no identifier begins
// with an angle bracket. So the tag alone decides which of the three shapes a line is, whatever a test
// printed.
export let TestProtocolBeganTag = ">"

// Marks a test that finished — see `TestProtocolBeganTag` for why the tag is a bracket.
export let TestProtocolEndedTag = "<"

// The ended-line outcome for a test whose body returned normally.
export let TestProtocolOutcomePass = "pass"

// The ended-line outcome for a test that threw. A test throws exactly `TestFailure` — a foreign error is
// converted by the handler the compiler substitutes, which reports it first — so this covers both a failed
// assertion and an uncaught foreign error, and the preceding `threw` line is what tells them apart.
export let TestProtocolOutcomeFail = "fail"

// ⭐ **THE OPTION THE BINARY READS ITS SELECTION FROM. SELECTION IS A RUNTIME ARGUMENT, NEVER A
// COMPILE-TIME ONE:** every discovered test is compiled in every time, which is what lets a `--filter`
// change leave the build cache warm and keeps the thing being tested identical to the thing that was
// compiled.
export let TestSelectOptionName = "select"

// The selection meaning "every test", used when the option is ABSENT — which is what a human running the
// binary by hand gets.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A DISTINCT TOKEN RATHER THAN THE EMPTY STRING BECAUSE THE RUNNER MUST BE ABLE TO SELECT
// NOTHING.** With absence and emptiness collapsed, a shard whose filter matched none of its tests would
// run all of them.
export let TestSelectAll = "*"

// Delimiter around each index in a selection, so a membership test is a plain substring search: `"|3|"`
// matches 3 and never 13 or 31. The generated dispatcher composes its needle from this constant directly,
// so the brackets it searches with are the brackets the runner writes.
export let TestSelectIndexBracket = "|"

// ⭐⭐ **THE FIELD SEPARATOR AS THE ONE BYTE A RUNNING BINARY PRINTS** — the pair to
// `TestProtocolSeparatorInMaxonSource` above, and the obligation that constant's own note recorded
// ("the reader half of the protocol needs the character and will declare it beside this constant").
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO ARE ONE FACT AT TWO MOMENTS AND MUST NEVER BE DERIVED FROM EACH OTHER.** Generated
// SOURCE carries the six-byte escape above, because it is text a compiler will lex; this is the
// single U+001F the compiled binary emits and the reader splits on. Deriving either from the other
// would mean un-escaping or re-escaping at run time — a third spelling of one byte, in the one place
// a mismatch is silent (every protocol line would simply parse as ordinary test output).
export let TestProtocolSeparator = "\u001f"

// ⭐ **THE SELECTION OPTION AS IT APPEARS IN argv.** Composed from `TestSelectOptionName` — the very
// name the generated binary passes to `CommandLine.getOptionValue` — so the flag the runner writes and
// the name the binary reads can never become two strings.
//
// A FUNCTION rather than a module `let`, because it INTERPOLATES: a top-level constant that allocates
// a String would run its initializer outside any test's ownership, which is why every other constant in
// this file is a literal (the shape `SpecTestRunner.wslProbeScript` already has).
export function testSelectFlag() returns String
	return "--{TestSelectOptionName}="
end 'testSelectFlag'

// ⭐⭐ **RENDER A SET OF RUN INDICES AS THE VALUE OF `--select=`** — the WRITER of the needle the
// generated dispatcher searches for, composed from the same `TestSelectIndexBracket` it brackets its
// search with. A selection that named test 3 and matched 13 would be exactly those two disagreeing.
//
// ⚠ **AN EMPTY LIST PRODUCES AN EMPTY VALUE, WHICH SELECTS NOTHING** — deliberately distinct from
// `TestSelectAll`. See that constant: with absence and emptiness collapsed, a shard whose filter
// matched none of its tests would run all of them.
export function formatTestSelection(indices TestRunIndexArray) returns String
	var value = ""
	for index in indices 'eachIndex'
		if value.isEmpty() 'openTheFirstBracket'
			value.append(TestSelectIndexBracket)
		end 'openTheFirstBracket'

		value.append("{index}{TestSelectIndexBracket}")
	end 'eachIndex'

	return value
end 'formatTestSelection'
